====================================================================
DOMAIN: Other German Idealist and Enlightenment Figures -- Fichte, Schelling, Herder, Schiller, Forster
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-06-27
PAPERS_FOUND: 15 total (High: 7, Medium: 6, Low: 2)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain covers the wider constellation of German Enlightenment and Idealist thinkers
who frame and complicate the Kant/Hegel colonialism debate. The central figure is Johann
Gottfried Herder (1744-1803), whose cultural pluralism, anti-Eurocentrism, and explicit
critiques of colonialism in the Ideen (1784-91) and Letters for the Advancement of Humanity
have positioned him as either the Enlightenment's most significant anti-imperialist voice
(Muthu, Noyes) or as a thinker whose cultural relativism remains entangled with racial and
civilizational hierarchies (Sikka's qualified assessment). The Kant-Herder controversy over
race and natural history -- in which Herder's Ideen challenged Kant's polygenist race theory
while Kant responded dismissively in his reviews -- is a key flashpoint in the literature.

A parallel set of debates concerns Georg Forster (1754-1794), the naturalist who accompanied
Cook on his second voyage and engaged Kant directly in the 1786-88 controversy over
monogenesis, race, and skin color. Forster's empiricist challenge to Kant's rationalist race
taxonomy, and Kant's refusal to engage Forster's challenge to oppose slavery, has become a
central case study in how Enlightenment anthropology related to colonial violence. Fichte's
Addresses to the German Nation (1807-8) raises different questions: whether his linguistic
and cultural nationalism harbors a "crypto-ethnic" exclusionism (Abizadeh) or a
"hypophysical" racism (Dwivedi) that, even without biological determinism, enabled
nationalist exclusion. Friedrich Schiller's aesthetic education project, while not primarily
about race, situates "savages" and "barbarians" as foils to the aesthetic ideal, implicitly
hierarchizing cultures along civilizational lines. Schelling's Naturphilosophie has received
less attention for race and colonialism but features in discussions of how German idealist
nature-philosophy contributed to organicist and racial thinking.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
These thinkers provide the comparative context for evaluating Kant and Hegel: Herder and
Forster mark the anti-imperialist possibility within the Enlightenment, showing that critics
of colonial hierarchies existed within the same intellectual milieu; Fichte shows how
post-Kantian idealism could generate ethnic-nationalist exclusion without explicit racist
biology; Schiller shows how aesthetic idealism mobilized the savage/barbarian contrast
internally while claiming universalism. Together they map the range of positions available
within German Idealism on questions of race, cultural difference, and empire.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Schelling's specific contribution to race-thinking within Naturphilosophie remains
underexplored in the secondary literature found here; dedicated monograph-length treatments
are absent from search results. The reception of Herder in colonial and postcolonial contexts
(Africa, Latin America) deserves more attention than the existing literature provides.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The Herder-Kant controversy and the Forster-Kant controversy should be read together as
showing how the Enlightenment's anti-colonial possibilities were actively suppressed by
Kant's dominant framework; the synthesis should resist treating Herder as simply "the good
one" by engaging Sikka's careful account of the limits of Herder's relativism.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Herder as anti-colonial: [4 papers] - Muthu, Noyes, Piirimae, Barnard defend Herder's anti-imperialism
- Herder as qualified/ambivalent: [3 papers] - Sikka, Denby, Niekerk show limits of his relativism
- Forster-Kant controversy: [3 papers] - Gray, Mensch, Van Gorkom map the eighteenth-century race debate
- Fichte's nationalism and exclusion: [2 papers] - Abizadeh, Dwivedi on crypto-ethnic nationalism
- Bernasconi's critical race methodology: [1 paper] - situates the whole German Enlightenment
- Schiller: [1 paper] - Leontiev on aesthetic education and its political ambiguities
====================================================================
}

@book{muthu2003enlightenment,
  author = {Muthu, Sankar},
  title = {Enlightenment against Empire},
  year = {2003},
  publisher = {Princeton University Press},
  address = {Princeton},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Muthu argues that a significant strand of Enlightenment thought -- centered on Diderot, Herder, and Kant -- constituted a systematic anti-imperial ideology grounded in a shared view of human agency and cultural diversity as inalienable goods. These thinkers attacked European empire not merely on grounds of injustice but because colonialism destroyed the very cultural plurality that makes human life meaningful.

  RELEVANCE: This book is the central reference point for the entire research project, establishing that the Enlightenment contained genuine anti-imperial resources. Its treatment of Herder is particularly important for Domain 5: Muthu positions Herder as the theorist who most fully worked out the philosophical implications of cultural diversity as a positive value. The book reframes the debate from "was the Enlightenment racist?" to "what were the internal tensions between Enlightenment universalism and anti-imperialism?"

  POSITION: Pro-anti-imperialist reading of Herder and the Enlightenment; defends the anti-colonial resources of German Idealism.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, Diderot, Kant, anti-imperialism, Enlightenment, colonialism, cultural-pluralism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{noyes2015herder,
  author = {Noyes, John K.},
  title = {Herder: Aesthetics against Imperialism},
  year = {2015},
  publisher = {University of Toronto Press},
  address = {Toronto},
  doi = {10.3138/9781442622975},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Noyes argues that Herder's anti-rationalist epistemology -- his rejection of universal conceptions of truth, knowledge, and justice -- constitutes the first sustained attempt to establish an epistemological (not merely moral) foundation for anti-imperialism. By refusing to grant any culture, including Enlightenment Europe, a monopoly on truth, Herder undermined the very epistemological claims that justified colonial domination.

  RELEVANCE: Directly bears on the question of whether Herder represents a genuine counter-current to the colonial implications of Enlightenment universalism. Noyes extends the argument beyond Muthu by focusing on the aesthetic and epistemological dimensions of Herder's anti-imperialism, connecting it to postcolonial theory (Chakrabarty, Spivak). Central for the section on Herder in this research project.

  POSITION: Strong anti-imperialist reading of Herder's aesthetics; connects Herder to postcolonial theory.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, aesthetics, anti-imperialism, postcolonial, epistemology, High},
  abstract = {Among his generation of intellectuals, the eighteenth-century German philosopher Johann Gottfried Herder is recognized both for his innovative philosophy of language and history and for his passionate criticism of racism, colonialism, and imperialism. A student of Immanuel Kant, Herder challenged the idea that anyone – even the philosophers of the Enlightenment – could have a monopoly on truth.In Herder: Aesthetics against Imperialism, John K. Noyes plumbs the connections between Herder’s anti-imperialism, often acknowledged but rarely explored in depth, and his epistemological investigations. Noyes argues that Herder’s anti-rationalist epistemology, his rejection of universal conceptions of truth, knowledge, and justice, constitutes the first attempt to establish not just a moral but an epistemological foundation for anti-imperialism. Engaging with the work of postcolonial theorists such Dipesh Chakrabarty and Gayatri Spivak, this book is a valuable reassessment of Enlightenment anti-imperialism that demonstrates Herder’s continuing relevance to postcolonial studies today},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{sikka2011herder,
  author = {Sikka, Sonia},
  title = {Herder on Humanity and Cultural Difference: Enlightened Relativism},
  year = {2011},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  address = {Cambridge},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9780511783012},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Sikka argues that Herder is best understood as a "qualified relativist" who combined a commitment to a common human nature with recognition of culture's role in shaping and differentiating that nature. Against simple relativist or universalist readings, Sikka shows that Herder's position is internally complex: he rejects racial hierarchy and Eurocentric teleology, but retains normative criteria for transcultural judgment that complicate pure cultural relativism.

  RELEVANCE: Essential for the research project because it resists both hagiographic anti-colonial readings of Herder (Muthu, Noyes) and dismissive ones. Sikka situates Herder's views on race within his anthropology, metaphysics, and ethics, showing that his qualified relativism opens space for anti-colonial critique without fully escaping Enlightenment universalism's ambiguities.

  POSITION: Nuanced, internally qualified reading of Herder's relativism; neither pure anti-imperialist nor complicit with colonialism.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, relativism, human-nature, cultural-difference, anthropology, High},
  abstract = {Herder is often criticized for having embraced cultural relativism, but there has been little philosophical discussion of what he actually wrote about the nature of the human species and its differentiation through culture. This book focuses on Herder's idea of culture, seeking to situate his social and political theses within the context of his anthropology, metaphysics, epistemology, ethics, theory of language and philosophy of history. It argues for a view of Herder as a qualified relativist, who combined the conception of a common human nature with a belief in the importance of culture in developing and shaping that nature. Especially highlighted are Herder's understanding of the relativity of virtue and happiness, and his belief in the impossibility of constructing a single best society. The book will appeal to a wide range of readers interested both in Herder and in Enlightenment culture more generally.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{barnard2003herder,
  author = {Barnard, F. M.},
  title = {Herder on Nationality, Humanity, and History},
  year = {2003},
  publisher = {McGill-Queen's University Press},
  address = {Montreal},
  doi = {10.1515/9780773570917},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Barnard demonstrates that Herder, despite his foundational role in theorizing nationality and cultural particularity, was explicitly aware of the dangers of ethnic fanaticism and opposed what we now call globalization. The core of Herder's nationalism lies in embedding human creativity in communal language while insisting on each culture's incommensurable distinctiveness within a broader humanity.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the canonical scholarly account of Herder's nationalism and its relationship to universalism, showing that Herder's nationalism was not protofascist but rather grounded in cultural pluralism. The book helps situate the question of whether Herder's emphasis on Volk and language anticipates the nationalist exclusions of Fichte's Addresses, or represents a fundamentally different logic.

  POSITION: Liberal-pluralist reading of Herder; distinguishes his nationalism from later ethnic nationalism.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, nationalism, humanity, cultural-pluralism, history, Medium},
  abstract = {The core of J.G. Herder's philosophy of nationalism lies in the conviction that human creativity must be embedded in the particular culture of a communal language. While he acknowledged that this cultural particular must be integrated into a more universal humanity, he insisted that each culture should preserve its incommensurable distinctiveness. He also called for a new method of enquiry regarding history, one that demands empathetic sensitivity toward the uniquely individual while realizing that there are few gains without losses.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{piirimae2023herder,
  author = {Piirim{\"a}e, Eva},
  title = {Herder and Enlightenment Politics},
  year = {2023},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  address = {Cambridge},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Piirimae reconstructs Herder's engagement with Rousseau, Montesquieu, Kant, and other contemporaries to show that Herder was deeply interested in the reform of modern European societies. She argues that Herder developed a distinctive understanding of human self-determination, natural sociability, and modern patriotism, and envisioned Europe as a commercially and culturally interconnected community of peoples rather than an imperial system.

  RELEVANCE: The most recent comprehensive study of Herder's political thought, providing essential context for evaluating Herder's anti-colonialism within his broader social and political vision. Piirimae's reconstruction of Herder's dialogue with Kant is directly relevant to the research project's concern with the Kant-Herder controversy. Published 2023.

  POSITION: Contextual-historical reading that situates Herder's anti-colonialism within his positive political program.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, Enlightenment, political-thought, anti-colonialism, Kant, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{niekerk2025herder,
  author = {Niekerk, Carl},
  title = {Herder's Concept of Culture},
  journal = {Monatshefte},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {117},
  number = {1},
  pages = {88--108},
  doi = {10.3368/m.117.1.88},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Niekerk traces the chronological development of Herder's concept of "culture," its Latin origins, links to theories of climate and geography, and its critical function in attacking Eurocentrism and colonialism. He asks how successful Herder is in avoiding the very cultural hierarchies he intends to criticize, arguing that the normative dimensions of the concept partially undercut his own anti-colonial intentions.

  RELEVANCE: This 2025 article provides the most recent critical assessment of whether Herder's anti-Eurocentrism is philosophically consistent or internally contradicted. The question of whether Herder's culture concept escapes hierarchical thinking is directly relevant to the project's central question about how German Enlightenment thinkers are implicated with colonialism even when they explicitly criticize it.

  POSITION: Critical reading that identifies internal tensions in Herder's anti-colonial project.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, culture, Eurocentrism, colonialism, anthropology, Medium},
  abstract = {This article presents a critical examination of the concept of “culture” in the writings of Johann Gottfried Herder. Herder’s use of the term “culture” participates in a broader pan-European rethinking of the term as part of the emerging discipline of anthropology. In a first step, the paper offers a chronological reconstruction of Herder’s use of the concept, its Latin origins, and its links to theories of climate and geography. As a second step, the essay examines the critical intentions underlying Herder’s theory of “culture,” in particular as part of a conceptual framework in his critiques of Eurocentrism and colonialism. Especially the normative dimensions of the concept are analyzed, and the question is asked how successful Herder is in avoiding the hierarchies he intends to criticize. Finally, the paper discusses the relevance of Herder’s theory of “culture” in the context of various anthropological approaches since the 1970s that have attempted to use “culture” as a critical tool in the analysis of contemporary globalization.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{gray2012kant,
  author = {Gray, Sally Hatch},
  title = {Kant's Race Theory, {Forster}'s Counter, and the Metaphysics of Color},
  journal = {The Eighteenth Century},
  year = {2012},
  volume = {53},
  number = {4},
  pages = {393--412},
  doi = {10.1353/ecy.2012.0032},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Gray analyzes the 1786-1788 controversy between Kant and Georg Forster over the metaphysical status of skin color as a racial characteristic. Kant treats color as a fixed hereditary marker of distinct races grounded in pre-formed germs; Forster's polygenist counter holds that skin color's meaning depends on local environments and cannot be extracted from lived cultural contexts. Gray argues the dispute turns on incompatible metaphysics of color.

  RELEVANCE: The Forster-Kant controversy is a crucial case study for the project because it shows how race theory in the Enlightenment was actively contested, and how Kant's racial taxonomy was philosophically vulnerable even by eighteenth-century standards. The metaphysical dimension Gray highlights shows that the debate was not merely empirical but tied to deep commitments about natural teleology.

  POSITION: Reconstructs the Forster-Kant dispute as a metaphysical controversy over the nature of racial characteristics.
  },
  keywords = {Forster, Kant, race-theory, skin-color, metaphysics, eighteenth-century, High},
  abstract = {This article argues for an understanding of Kant's race theory as an integral part of his idea of nature and of humans in nature as presented in his Critique of the Power of Judgment (Kritik der Urteilskraft, 1790). It places an examination of Kant and Forster's debate over race, which was ignited in 1785 upon the publication of Kant's second essay on race, "Definition of a Concept of a Human Race" ("Bestimmung des Begriffs einer Menschenrace"), in the context of an illumination of the connections between aesthetics and anthropology in Kant's Observations on the Feeling of the Beautiful and Sublime (Beobachtungen über das Gefühl des Schönen und Erhabenen, 1764) and Forster's Voyage round the World (Reise um die Welt 1777). Forster responded to Kant's new "race" classifications, which were based essentially on skin color, with "Still More about the Human Races" ("Noch etwas über Menschenraßen," 1786). This article shows that Kant then developed his scientific theory and his idea of a teleological nature as presented in his Critique of the Power of Judgment, at least in part, in order to provide a unifying theoretical basis for his race theory so that it could withstand the scrutiny of an empirical scientific method based on deductive logic, such as that advanced by Forster. While Forster's strict empiricism, perspectivism, and rejection of "race" as a scientific classification reflect an underlying, distinctly modern, concept of the natural world, Kant's nature, as presented in his third Critique, reveals a metaphysically-based structure supporting a universal cosmopolitanism, but veils a particular European perspective that allows a damaging global authority on difference.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{mensch2025kant,
  author = {Mensch, Jennifer},
  title = {Kant and {Forster} on the Unity of Mankind},
  journal = {Annals of Science},
  year = {2025},
  pages = {1--23},
  doi = {10.1080/00033790.2025.2584331},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Mensch widens the frame of the Forster-Kant race controversy by bringing Kant's Physical Geography lectures into conversation with Forster's travel writings from Cook's second voyage. She shows that the two thinkers share more common ground as ethnographers than usually recognized, while their genuine divergences on monogenesis and racial hierarchy reflect deeper commitments to empiricism (Forster) versus rational teleology (Kant).

  RELEVANCE: The most recent scholarly treatment (2025) of the Forster-Kant controversy, which is central to Domain 5's focus. Mensch's inclusion of Forster's other 1780s writings broadens the picture beyond the formal academic debate, showing Forster as a naturalist-traveler whose anti-hierarchical anthropology emerges from direct encounter with Pacific peoples.

  POSITION: Comparative-contextualist reading that enriches the Forster-Kant debate with new archival and contextual material.
  },
  keywords = {Forster, Kant, race, unity-of-mankind, anthropology, natural-history, High},
  abstract = {In 1786 Georg Forster published a widely read critique of Immanuel Kant's theory of race. Since then, the dispute between Forster and Kant on the unity of mankind has been widely discussed in light of both Forster's essay and Kant's decision to write a lengthy response to Forster in 1788. In this discussion I widen the frame for considering the two positions by focusing on Kant's lectures on Physical Geography. In these notes Kant emerges as an ethnographer asking many of the same questions posed by Forster himself, a man who had become famous since his time spent onboard James Cook's second voyage to the South Pacific (1772-1775). Placing Kant's ethnography in closer conversation with Forster reveals the many similarities (and some well-known differences) between the two. By including some of Forster's other writings from the 1780s in an assessment of their debate, moreover, a much fuller picture can be had regarding natural historical investigations into the unity and difference of mankind at the time.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{vangorkom2022stoll,
  author = {Van Gorkom, Joris},
  title = {Johann Gottlieb {Stoll} and {Forster}'s Challenge to {Kant}},
  journal = {Critical Philosophy of Race},
  year = {2022},
  volume = {10},
  number = {2},
  pages = {295--311},
  doi = {10.5325/critphilrace.10.2.0295},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Van Gorkom recovers the largely forgotten figure of Johann Gottlieb Stoll, who in response to Forster's 1786 challenge to Kant went further than both by explicitly condemning slavery and the slave trade on grounds of human dignity, and by arguing that racial mixing proves the unity of the species and is not contrary to nature. Stoll presented an even more radical challenge to Kant than Forster did.

  RELEVANCE: Illuminates how the Forster-Kant controversy generated further anti-racist positions in the Enlightenment context. Stoll's defense of racial mixing and condemnation of slavery shows that abolitionist positions were available within the German Enlightenment conversation about race, making Kant's silence on these matters more culpable rather than simply uninformed.

  POSITION: Critical philosophy of race perspective; uses the Forster-Kant controversy to expand the cast of anti-racist voices in the German Enlightenment.
  },
  keywords = {Forster, Kant, Stoll, race, slavery, monogenesis, eighteenth-century, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract:When in 1786 Georg Forster criticized Immanuel Kant’s racial theory, he famously challenged him to oppose slavery. Although Kant declined to take up this challenge, the discussion between Forster and Kant was the impetus for Johann Gottlieb Stoll to present his views on the matter. In his defense of monogenesis Stoll did what Kant had failed to do, namely, explicitly criticize oppressive institutions like the slave trade and slavery with a demand to respect the dignity and humanity of every human being, independent of their physiological traits. Although his work has been ignored ever since its publication, he offered an even greater challenge to Kant than Forster. He defended the idea that so-called race mixing not only proved the unity of the human species but was also not contrary to nature. Not only did he distance himself from Kant but also from Forster. He challenged his readers to consider the ability of non-Whites to be an example for Whites.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{vangorkom2024metzger,
  author = {Van Gorkom, Joris},
  title = {Johann Daniel {Metzger} and {Kant}'s So-Called Human Races},
  journal = {Journal for Eighteenth-Century Studies},
  year = {2024},
  doi = {10.1111/1754-0208.12946},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Van Gorkom recovers the contributions of Johann Daniel Metzger to the 1780s race debate -- contributions listed by Blumenbach but completely ignored by subsequent scholarship. Metzger offered an early critical engagement with Kant's concept of race that broadens our understanding of eighteenth-century debates beyond the dominant Herder-Forster-Kant triangle.

  RELEVANCE: Expands the Kant-Herder-Forster race controversy to include Metzger, showing that the debate was richer and more contested than usually presented. Relevant to the project's concern with how widely contested Kant's race theory was among his contemporaries, and how many alternative positions existed within the German Enlightenment.

  POSITION: Historical recovery of under-studied participants in the Kant race debate.
  },
  keywords = {Metzger, Kant, race, Blumenbach, eighteenth-century, natural-history, Medium},
  abstract = {Around 1788, Johann Friedrich Blumenbach compiled a list of notable contributions to the discussion on Kant's concept of race. This list included references to Kant's second and third essays on race, Georg Forster's 1786 response, and two essays by Johann Daniel Metzger. Blumenbach's compilation stands in stark contrast to contemporary discussions of Kant's racial theories. Despite the increasing interest in this subject, Metzger's critiques are still largely unnoticed. To gain a more comprehensive understanding of eighteenth‐century discussions on race, it is crucial to broaden our perspective beyond the viewpoints of Johann Gottfried Herder and Forster and include Metzger's contributions. In this article, I aim to elucidate (the context surrounding) Metzger's endeavours to engage with Kant's concept of race.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{abizadeh2005fichte,
  author = {Abizadeh, Arash},
  title = {Was {Fichte} an Ethnic Nationalist? On Cultural Nationalism and Its Double},
  journal = {History of Political Thought},
  year = {2005},
  volume = {26},
  number = {2},
  pages = {334--359},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Abizadeh argues that while the dominant French interpretation reads Fichte's Addresses to the German Nation as a non-ethnic cultural nationalism, this misses the text's "crypto-ethnic" character. Fichte's nationalism, though not based on biological race, smuggles in ethno-cultural exclusion through the criterion of "original" versus "dead" languages, effectively naturalizing national community through language while excluding non-German-speakers.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the question of whether Fichte's nationalism entails exclusion and, implicitly, racially inflected hierarchy. The "crypto-ethnic" concept is crucial for situating Fichte within the project's concern about how German Idealism normalized national and racial exclusions without always making them explicit. The article is the standard critical reference on Fichte's nationalism.

  POSITION: Critical reading that identifies ethnic-nationalist implications in Fichte's ostensibly cultural nationalism.
  },
  keywords = {Fichte, nationalism, ethnicity, language, exclusion, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{dwivedi2022hypophysics,
  author = {Dwivedi, Divya},
  title = {The Hypophysics of Philosophical Nationalism},
  journal = {Eco-ethica},
  year = {2022},
  volume = {10},
  pages = {43--60},
  doi = {10.5840/ecoethica202342652},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Dwivedi argues that Fichte's nationalism in the Addresses constitutes a specific form of racism she calls "hypophysics" -- a consecration of nature as value in which language, community, and race flow from a single "spiritual-natural law." This hypophysical logic is distinct from conventional naturalism or biologism, and from Heidegger's onto-theo-logic, yet it underwrites racial exclusion through the spiritual-natural unity of an "original" Volk.

  RELEVANCE: Offers the sharpest critical diagnosis of how Fichte's nationalism generates racial thinking without explicit racial biology. The concept of "hypophysics" is a productive analytical tool for the project's engagement with how German Idealism normalized exclusion. The article also poses the question of whether philosophy as such can exist without hypophysics and its attendant racisms.

  POSITION: Radical critique identifying a structural racism in Fichte's philosophical nationalism.
  },
  keywords = {Fichte, nationalism, race, language, hypophysics, philosophy, Medium},
  abstract = {Is there a philosophical nationalism? Reading Fichte’s Addresses to the German Nation and its use by the Nazis, Derrida concluded that all nationalisms are philosophical and onto-theological as they are posited beyond race, biology, and nature. However, Fichte’s text reveals a specific form of racism that insists on biology and nature. Fichte’s racism is a species of “hypophysics,” a consecration of nature as value. His theory of language is simultaneously biological and spiritual, these two aspects flowing from a single geistige Naturgesetz (spiritual-natural-law) and determining the hypophysical unity of language, community and “philosophy” (as he defined it). The logic of such a hypophysics is misrecognized and left unaddressed in the conventional categories of “naturalism” and “biologism.” Further, hypophysical-logic is not onto-theo-logic (in Heidegger’s definition). This imposes new questions for the history and future of philosophy: how does hypophysics enter philosophy; can there be philosophy without hypophysics and its attendant racisms?},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@incollection{bernasconi2020invented,
  author = {Bernasconi, Robert},
  title = {Who Invented the Concept of Race?},
  booktitle = {Theories of Race and Racism},
  year = {2020},
  pages = {83--103},
  publisher = {Routledge},
  doi = {10.4324/9781003060802-7},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Bernasconi argues that Kant invented the modern concept of race as a technical biological-philosophical category in his 1775 and 1785 essays, establishing race as a hereditary, fixed characteristic serving the teleological purposes of natural history. Against Herder, who treated human diversity as cultural and environmental rather than racial, Kant insisted on a strict racial classification that made biological race a permanent, inherited characteristic for the first time.

  RELEVANCE: The claim that Kant invented race directly implicates Herder as Kant's main opponent on this question -- Herder's rejection of fixed racial categories becomes part of the evidence that Kant's race concept was a philosophical innovation rather than a neutral description of nature. This chapter is foundational for the project because it sets the terms for understanding what was at stake in the Kant-Herder controversy.

  POSITION: Critical philosophy of race; attributes the invention of modern race concept to Kant rather than to any biological naturalist.
  },
  keywords = {Bernasconi, Kant, Herder, race, invention, natural-history, High},
  abstract = {WHO Invented the Concept of Race? This question admits of a variety of answers depending on what one takes to be decisive about the concept and on whether one believes that what defines the moment in which a technical term is introduced is the first usage of the word in the required sense or the definition that secures its status and influence. The terms and basis of the definition might continue to be scrutinized, but, so long as the term was being used only loosely, it made no sense to contest the concept. The invention of the concept of race in this sense took place some time after the introduction of the broad division of peoples on the basis of color, nationality, and other inherited characteristics that could not be overcome subsequently, as religious differences could be overcome by conversion. A word should also be said about Blumenbach’s claim to being the originator of the anthropological concept of race.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{leontiev2023schiller,
  author = {Leontiev, Kim},
  title = {Schiller on Aesthetic Education as Radical Ethical-Political Remedy},
  journal = {British Journal of Aesthetics},
  year = {2023},
  doi = {10.1093/aesthj/ayad003},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Leontiev examines Schiller's Letters on the Aesthetic Education of Man as a response to the perceived failures of Kantian ethics and Enlightenment republicanism. Schiller diagnoses "savage" and "barbarian" states of the human as failures of aesthetic balance -- the savage dominated by sense, the barbarian by abstract reason -- and proposes aesthetic experience as the remedy for political fragmentation. The political dimension of this aesthetic anthropology receives systematic analysis.

  RELEVANCE: For the project, the article is relevant because Schiller's "savage/barbarian" typology maps onto contemporary colonial categories and implicitly hierarchizes non-European peoples (especially Pacific Islanders and indigenous Americans) as aesthetically deficient. Leontiev's focus on the political remedy dimension helps connect Schiller's aesthetic anthropology to questions of colonial paternalism and civilizational hierarchy.

  POSITION: Sympathetic but analytically rigorous treatment of Schiller's aesthetico-political project, relevant to its colonial implications.
  },
  keywords = {Schiller, aesthetic-education, savage, barbarian, politics, Kant, Medium},
  abstract = {
  abstract_source = {s2},
 This paper examines the iconic conception of aesthetic education in the work of Friedrich Schiller, with the aim of elucidating Schiller’s unique innovation of this notion in understanding i) the relationship between aesthetic and ethical value and ii) the transformative possibilities within a collective, social dimension of aesthetic experience. The paper provides an overview of the Kantian origins of Schiller’s aesthetic programme (Section 1). It then considers Schiller’s critique of the perceived failings of the Kantian and Enlightenment republican models of ethical value and political emancipation (Section 2) before turning to his positive alternative aesthetic programme (Section 3). The paper concludes (Section 4) with some evaluation and reflection on Schiller’s original contributions.},
